1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 PURPOSE

The purpose of Orgyo is to provide a centralized web-based platform that helps users discover, filter, evaluate, and compare colleges and educational institutes according to their individual preferences, requirements, and priorities.
Selecting a suitable college can be a difficult and time-consuming process because students and parents often need to collect information from multiple websites, compare different colleges manually, depend on reviews, and sometimes visit colleges physically before making a decision. Different students may also have different priorities when selecting a college. For example, one student may prioritize distance and fees, while another may prioritize placement performance, technical events, infrastructure, faculty qualifications, academic curriculum, research opportunities, or campus environment.
Orgyo is designed to address this problem by allowing users to define the factors that are important to them and assign suitable preferences or priorities to those factors. Based on the user's requirements, the system will identify and present colleges that match the user's specified constraints and preferences.
The system will allow users to:
•	Search for colleges and courses based on their requirements.
•	Specify personal preferences and priorities for different college-related parameters.
•	Define constraints such as maximum distance, preferred location, course, fee range, and other requirements.
•	Filter colleges according to specified constraints.
•	Compare colleges using multiple parameters.
•	Give different levels of priority to different parameters.
•	View relevant information about colleges, courses, faculty, infrastructure, academic curriculum, placement performance, fees, campus environment, technical events, competitions, and other relevant factors.
•	View information and feedback contributed by current students, alumni, faculty, staff, and other individuals associated with the respective college, subject to the verification mechanism of Orgyo.
•	View information collected from official, public, and other permitted external sources.
•	Identify the source and verification status of available information where applicable.
•	Evaluate colleges based on information relevant to their individual requirements.
The data used by Orgyo may be collected from multiple sources. These sources may include current students, alumni, faculty, staff, and other individuals associated with a college, as well as official college websites, public databases, government sources, and other permitted external sources.
Orgyo will distinguish information based on its source and, where applicable, its verification status. Information provided by verified contributors may be given greater relevance in appropriate areas of the system, while external and official information will be maintained according to its respective source.
The system will not determine an objectively "best" college for every user. Instead, Orgyo will determine and display colleges that are more closely aligned with the user's specified requirements and priorities. The final decision regarding the selection of a college will remain with the user.
The initial version of Orgyo will primarily focus on college and educational institute discovery. However, the platform is intended to be extensible, allowing additional preference-based discovery and decision-support features to be introduced in future versions. These may include job-seeker support, organization discovery, career opportunities, and solutions for other similar real-world problems where users need to find suitable options according to their individual requirements.

1.2 SCOPE

This Software Requirements Specification (SRS) document defines the scope and requirements of Orgyo, a web-based college discovery, comparison, and preference-based matching platform.
The primary scope of Orgyo is to assist students, particularly students who have completed or are completing their 12th standard, in finding colleges and educational institutes according to their individual requirements and priorities.
Unlike a system that simply displays a general list or ranking of colleges, Orgyo will allow users to define what factors are important to them. These factors may include, but are not limited to:
•	Distance from the user's preferred location.
•	Course availability.
•	College fees.
•	Placement performance.
•	Academic curriculum.
•	Faculty qualifications and achievements.
•	Infrastructure.
•	Technical and academic environment.
•	Frequency of technical events.
•	Hackathons and competitions.
•	Internship opportunities.
•	Research opportunities.
•	Campus environment.
•	Student activities.
•	Hostel and accommodation facilities.
•	Other user-defined requirements.
For example, a user may specify a maximum distance of 10 kilometres from their preferred location, a particular course, a maximum fee limit, and high priorities for technical events, faculty quality, academic curriculum, infrastructure, and placement performance.
Another user may assign different priorities, such as low fees, hostel facilities, campus environment, location, and placement opportunities.
Therefore, the results generated by Orgyo may differ from one user to another based on their individual requirements and preferences.

1.2.1 DATA SOURCES
Orgyo will use information collected from multiple sources to build and maintain college profiles. These sources may include:
1.	Current students of the respective college.
2.	Alumni or graduated students of the respective college.
3.	Faculty members, staff, or other individuals associated with the respective college.
4.	Official college and university websites.
5.	Government and publicly available educational databases.
6.	Publicly available information from permitted external sources.
7.	Other legitimate and permitted sources containing relevant college-related information.
User-contributed information will be associated with the contributor's verification status wherever applicable. The system will also maintain information about the source of externally obtained data where required.

1.2.2 PREFERENCE-BASED COLLEGE MATCHING
The core functionality of Orgyo is preference-based college discovery and matching.
The system will allow users to define:
•	Mandatory requirements or constraints.
•	Preferred values or ranges.
•	Relative priorities for different parameters.
Mandatory requirements may include conditions such as:
•	Maximum distance from a specified location.
•	Maximum or minimum fee.
•	Required course.
•	Preferred location.
•	Required facilities.
•	Other user-defined constraints.
Preference-based parameters may include:
•	Placement performance.
•	Faculty quality.
•	Faculty achievements.
•	Infrastructure.
•	Academic curriculum.
•	Technical environment.
•	Technical events.
•	Competitions and hackathons.
•	Research opportunities.
•	Campus environment.
•	Internship opportunities.
•	Other relevant parameters.
The system will use these requirements and preferences to filter and evaluate available colleges and present the user with colleges that match their specified criteria.
The matching process will not represent an absolute ranking of colleges. It will represent the suitability of available colleges according to the specific preferences and requirements provided by the user.

1.2.3 COLLEGE COMPARISON
Orgyo will allow users to compare multiple colleges based on selected parameters.
The comparison may include factors such as:
•	Distance.
•	Fees.
•	Courses.
•	Placement performance.
•	Faculty qualifications and achievements.
•	Infrastructure.
•	Academic curriculum.
•	Technical events.
•	Competitions and hackathons.
•	Internship opportunities.
•	Campus environment.
•	Other relevant parameters.
The comparison feature will allow users to understand the differences between colleges and make their own final decision.

1.2.4 INFORMATION AND VERIFICATION
Orgyo will provide information from both community contributors and external sources.
Community contributors may include current students, alumni, faculty, staff, and other individuals associated with a college.
Where possible, Orgyo will verify the association of contributors with the respective college. The system will identify the verification status of contributors and information where applicable.
Verification will not necessarily mean that an opinion or statement is objectively true. Instead, it will indicate that the system has established an association between the contributor and the respective college or organization according to the defined verification process.

1.2.5 FUTURE EXPANSION
The initial implementation of Orgyo will focus on college discovery, comparison, and preference-based matching.
The architecture and overall concept of the platform may be extended in future versions to address other areas where users need to discover and evaluate suitable options according to their individual requirements.
Possible future expansions may include:
1.	Job and career opportunity discovery based on user preferences, skills, qualifications, location, salary expectations, and other requirements.
2.	Organization and company discovery based on user-defined criteria.
3.	Career and professional opportunity matching.
4.	Additional decision-support solutions for other real-world problems that can be addressed through preference-based search, comparison, verification, and matching.
These features are considered future enhancements and are not part of the core requirements of the initial version unless explicitly included in a later version of the system.

1.3 OUT OF SCOPE

The following functionalities and responsibilities are outside the scope of the initial version of Orgyo:
1.	Orgyo will not guarantee admission to any college, university, or educational institute.
2.	Orgyo will not conduct or manage the official admission process of any college or university.
3.	Orgyo will not make admission decisions on behalf of colleges or educational institutions.
4.	Orgyo will not guarantee that a particular college is the best choice for every user.
5.	Orgyo will not guarantee that a user will receive admission to a college displayed by the system.
6.	Orgyo will not replace the need for physical visits where physical verification, counselling, documentation, or other activities are required.
7.	Orgyo will not guarantee the accuracy of every user-generated opinion or experience.
8.	Verification of a contributor will not automatically mean that every statement or opinion provided by that contributor is factually correct.
9.	Orgyo will not make decisions on behalf of students regarding their final college selection.
10.	Orgyo will not directly conduct college admissions, examinations, counselling, or document verification.
11.	Job-search, recruitment, and career-matching functionality will not be part of the initial implementation unless specifically included in a future version of the system.
12.	Orgyo will not guarantee employment, internships, job selection, or any other career outcome through future job-related features.

1.4 DEFINITIONS, ACRONYMS AND ABBREVIATIONS

1.4.1 Definitions
Orgyo:
A web-based college discovery, comparison, and preference-based matching platform designed to help users identify educational institutes according to their individual requirements and priorities.
User:
A person who accesses and uses Orgyo to search, filter, compare, evaluate, or provide information related to colleges and courses.
Student:
A user who primarily uses Orgyo to search for colleges, universities, courses, and other educational opportunities.
College:
An educational institution that provides undergraduate, postgraduate, diploma, or other academic programs.
Institute:
An educational institution such as a college, university, training institute, or other organization providing educational programs.
Course:
An academic, professional, technical, or educational program offered by a college or institute.
Preference:
A factor or characteristic that a user considers important when selecting a college.
Priority:
The relative level of importance assigned by a user to a particular preference or parameter.
Constraint:
A mandatory condition that a college must satisfy to be considered by the system.
Parameter:
A measurable or evaluative factor used to describe, filter, compare, or evaluate a college.
College Match:
The degree to which a college satisfies the requirements and preferences specified by a particular user.
Verified Contributor:
A user whose association with a particular college or institute has been verified through the verification mechanism defined by Orgyo.
Community Data:
Information, feedback, experience, or other college-related data contributed by current students, alumni, faculty, staff, or other individuals associated with a college.
External Data:
Information obtained from official websites, government databases, public datasets, or other permitted external sources.
Verification:
The process of confirming the association or source of a contributor or information according to the verification mechanism implemented by Orgyo.
Feedback:
An experience, observation, opinion, review, or other information provided by a user regarding a college, course, faculty, infrastructure, campus environment, placement, or other relevant factor.
Comparison:
The process of evaluating two or more colleges using selected parameters or criteria.
Matching:
The process of evaluating available colleges against a user's specified constraints and preferences to identify suitable results.
Future Enhancement:
A feature or functionality that is planned or considered for a future version of Orgyo but is not included in the requirements of the initial implementation.

1.4.2 Acronyms and Abbreviations
SRS: Software Requirements Specification
UI: User Interface
UX: User Experience
API: Application Programming Interface
DB: Database
DBMS: Database Management System
OTP: One-Time Password
URL: Uniform Resource Locator
AI: Artificial Intelligence
ML: Machine Learning

1.5 REFERENCES
The following references and resources may be considered during the analysis, design, development, and documentation of the Orgyo system:
1.	IEEE/ISO/IEC guidelines and standards related to Software Requirements Specifications.
2.	Official websites of colleges, universities, and educational institutions for publicly available academic and institutional information.
3.	Official course, fee, curriculum, faculty, infrastructure, and placement information published by educational institutions.
4.	Government and publicly available educational datasets and databases.
5.	Publicly available information from permitted external sources.
6.	Software engineering and web application development documentation.
7.	Technical documentation of the programming languages, frameworks, databases, APIs, and other technologies used to develop Orgyo.

1.6 OVERVIEW

This Software Requirements Specification document describes the requirements, scope, functionality, constraints, assumptions, and overall characteristics of the Orgyo web application.
Section 1 introduces Orgyo and defines its purpose, scope, data sources, major concepts, definitions, acronyms, references, and overall structure.
The subsequent sections of this document will describe the overall system, system users and their roles, major system functions, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, external interfaces, data requirements, preference-based matching, verification mechanisms, system constraints, assumptions, dependencies, and other requirements necessary for the development and implementation of Orgyo.
The primary objective of Orgyo is to provide a preference-based college discovery and comparison platform in which users can define what matters to them and receive college results based on their individual requirements rather than relying solely on general rankings or predefined recommendations.
Orgyo will combine information from community contributors and external sources and use that information to support college discovery, filtering, comparison, and preference-based matching. The system will present relevant information and its available source or verification status so that users can independently evaluate the results and make their final decision.
The platform will be designed with future extensibility in mind. Although the initial version will focus on educational institute discovery and matching, the underlying concept may later be extended to job discovery, career opportunities, organization discovery, and other preference-based decision-support solutions.

2. OVERALL DESCRIPTION
2.1 Product Perspective

Orgyo is a web-based college discovery, comparison, and preference-based matching platform. The system is designed to help students and other users discover and evaluate colleges and educational institutes according to their individual requirements, preferences, and priorities.

The system will collect and organize information from multiple sources, including official college websites, government and publicly available educational databases, current students, alumni, faculty, staff, and other permitted external sources.

Orgyo will provide search, filtering, comparison, preference-based matching, and information verification features. The system will not determine an objectively “best” college for every user. Instead, it will present colleges according to the requirements and priorities specified by each individual user.

2.1.1 System Interfaces

Orgyo may interact with the following external systems and services:

Database System: Used to store user, college, course, preference, feedback, and verification data.
External Data Sources: Used to obtain publicly available college and educational information.
Authentication Services: Used for user authentication, where applicable.
External APIs: May be used to obtain permitted educational, geographical, or other relevant information.
Web Browsers: Used by users to access and interact with the Orgyo platform.

The exact external interfaces will depend on the technologies and services selected during the implementation of the system.

2.1.2 User Interfaces

Orgyo shall provide a web-based user interface through which users can:

Register and log in to the system.
Search for colleges and courses.
Specify preferences and priorities.
Define mandatory constraints.
Filter available colleges.
Compare multiple colleges.
View college and course information.
View information sources and verification status, where applicable.
Provide feedback or information according to their respective user roles.

The user interface shall be designed to provide simple, consistent, and easy navigation for users with basic knowledge of web applications.

2.1.3 Hardware Interfaces

Orgyo does not require specialized hardware for normal users.

The system shall be accessible through standard computing devices, including:

Desktop computers
Laptop computers
Tablets
Smartphones

The server-side hardware shall provide sufficient processing power, memory, and storage capacity to operate the application, database, and associated services.

2.1.4 Software Interfaces

Orgyo will interact with the following software components:

Web browsers
Web/application server
Database Management System
Backend application framework
Frontend technologies
External APIs or services, where applicable
Authentication and security components

The specific technologies used for implementation may be finalized during the system design and development stages.

2.1.5 Communications Interfaces

Orgyo shall use standard web communication protocols for communication between users, the application server, database, and permitted external services.

The system may use:

HTTP/HTTPS: For communication between the client and server.
REST APIs: For communication with external services, where required.
TCP/IP: As the underlying network communication protocol.

HTTPS shall be preferred for secure communication between the client and server.

2.1.6 Memory Constraints

The system shall operate within the available memory and storage resources of the client and server environments.

The system should be designed to:

Manage college and course information efficiently.
Minimize unnecessary memory consumption.
Support multiple users without excessive resource usage.
Store application and database data efficiently.

The exact memory and storage requirements will depend on the final deployment environment and expected system load.

2.1.7 Operations

The following operational activities shall be supported or managed by the system:

User registration and authentication.
College and course information management.
Data collection and updating.
Preference and matching operations.
Information verification.
User feedback management.
Database backup and maintenance.
System performance monitoring.
Administrative management of system data and users.

Administrators shall be responsible for authorized management and maintenance activities.

2.1.8 Site Adaptation Requirements

The system should be adaptable to different educational institutions, locations, and deployment environments.

Site-specific configuration may include:

Location and distance information.
Currency and fee formats.
Date and time formats.
Educational terminology.
Institution-specific information.
Language support in future versions.
Configuration of data sources and verification mechanisms.
2.2 Product Functions

The major functions of Orgyo shall include the following:

2.2.1 User Management

The system shall provide:

User registration and login.
User profile management.
Management of user preferences and requirements.
2.2.2 College and Course Search

The system shall allow users to:

Search for colleges.
Search for courses.
Search according to user-defined requirements.
2.2.3 Preference Management

The system shall allow users to:

Define preferred parameters.
Assign different priority levels to parameters.
Define mandatory requirements and constraints.
2.2.4 College Filtering

The system shall allow users to filter colleges according to criteria such as:

Distance
Fees
Course availability
Location
Facilities
Other user-defined requirements
2.2.5 Preference-Based Matching

The system shall:

Evaluate colleges against user-defined constraints and preferences.
Identify colleges that satisfy mandatory requirements.
Present colleges that closely match the user's specified preferences.

The matching process shall represent suitability according to the user's requirements rather than an absolute ranking of colleges.

2.2.6 College Comparison

The system shall allow users to compare multiple colleges using parameters such as:

Distance
Fees
Courses
Placement performance
Faculty qualifications and achievements
Infrastructure
Academic curriculum
Technical events
Competitions and hackathons
Internship opportunities
Campus environment
2.2.7 College Information

The system shall provide relevant information about:

Colleges
Courses
Faculty
Infrastructure
Academic curriculum
Placement performance
Fees
Campus environment
Technical events
Competitions
Internship opportunities
Research opportunities
2.2.8 Information and Verification

The system shall:

Display the source of available information where applicable.
Display contributor verification status.
Distinguish community-contributed information from externally obtained information.
Maintain information about the source of externally obtained data where required.

Verification of a contributor shall indicate an established association with the respective college or organization and shall not necessarily indicate that every opinion or statement provided by that contributor is factually correct.

2.2.9 Feedback

The system shall allow eligible users to provide relevant feedback, experiences, observations, opinions, or other information regarding colleges, courses, faculty, infrastructure, campus environment, placements, and related factors.

2.2.10 Administrative Management

Administrators shall be able to manage:

Users
College information
Course information
Contributed information
Verification processes
Feedback
Other system data
2.3 User Characteristics

Orgyo is intended for users who need to discover, compare, and evaluate colleges and educational institutes.

2.3.1 Students

Students are the primary users of Orgyo. They may use the system to:

Search for colleges and courses.
Define their requirements.
Set preferences and priorities.
Filter colleges.
Compare colleges.
Evaluate available information.

The primary target group includes students who have completed or are completing their 12th standard.

2.3.2 Parents or Guardians

Parents or guardians may use Orgyo to:

Explore colleges.
Compare educational institutions.
Review fees, location, facilities, placements, and other information.
Support students during the college selection process.
2.3.3 Community Contributors

Current students, alumni, faculty, staff, and other individuals associated with a college may contribute relevant information or feedback.

Where applicable, the system may verify their association with the respective college or institute.

2.3.4 Administrators

Administrators shall be responsible for authorized system management activities, including:

User management.
College and course information management.
Data management.
Verification processes.
Feedback management.
System maintenance.
2.4 Constraints

The development and operation of Orgyo may be subject to the following constraints:

The system depends on the availability and quality of information obtained from different sources.
External data sources may change their structure, availability, or access policies.
User-generated information may not always be objectively accurate.
Verification of a contributor does not guarantee that every statement or opinion provided by that contributor is factually correct.
The system shall not guarantee admission to any college or educational institute.
The system shall not conduct or manage the official admission process of any college or university.
The system shall not make the final college selection decision on behalf of the user.
System performance may be affected by available hardware, database capacity, network connectivity, and server resources.
Security and privacy requirements shall be considered when handling user accounts and contributed information.
The initial version shall primarily focus on college discovery, comparison, and preference-based matching.
2.5 Assumptions and Dependencies
2.5.1 Assumptions

The development and operation of Orgyo are based on the following assumptions:

Users will have access to an internet-enabled device.
Users will have access to a modern web browser.
Users will provide reasonably accurate preferences and requirements.
Relevant college information will be available from official, public, community, or other permitted sources.
Users will understand that matching results represent suitability according to their specified requirements and are not absolute rankings.
Administrators will maintain the system and manage authorized information and verification processes.
2.5.2 Dependencies

The system may depend on:

Availability of internet connectivity.
Availability of the application server.
Availability of the database server.
Availability of permitted external data sources.
Availability of external APIs, where applicable.
Web browser compatibility.
Third-party authentication or communication services, if implemented.
Availability of updated college and educational information.
2.6 Apportioning of Requirements

The initial version of Orgyo shall primarily focus on the following functionalities:

College discovery.
Course discovery.
Preference-based college matching.
College filtering.
College comparison.
Information and source identification.
Contributor verification.
User feedback.

The following features may be considered for future versions of the system:

Job and career opportunity discovery.
Job matching based on skills, qualifications, location, salary expectations, and user preferences.
Organization and company discovery.
Career and professional opportunity matching.
Additional verification and decision-support features.
Other preference-based solutions for real-world problems.

These features shall be considered future enhancements and shall not be included in the core requirements of the initial version unless explicitly specified in a later version of the SRS. This is consistent with your existing scope, where job/career and other preference-based extensions are identified as future functionality.
